WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.434 --> 00:00:05.801
-I started last "Corrections"
saying,

00:00:05.801 --> 00:00:07.300
"Last 'Corrections' in March."

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.200
And a lot of you pointed out
when you saw it,

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.634
it was actually April 1st.

00:00:10.634 --> 00:00:11.667
Gotcha.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.133
[ Light laughter ]

00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:18.334
Brontosaurus --

00:00:18.334 --> 00:00:20.267
So this is interesting.
I said dinosaurs --

00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:21.300
I was talking about dinosaurs,

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.267
said brontosaurus,
said pterodactyl.

00:00:23.267 --> 00:00:26.868
A lot of feedback
from those of you in the know

00:00:26.868 --> 00:00:29.167
re: dinosaurs.

00:00:29.167 --> 00:00:31.901
Brontosaurus,
it's apatosaurus, I guess,

00:00:31.901 --> 00:00:35.634
is the correct name
for what we --

00:00:35.634 --> 00:00:38.033
When we see a brontosaurus,
it's actually an apatosaurus.

00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:44.434
But then in 2015, they did say
brontosaurus was okay.

00:00:44.434 --> 00:00:46.400
So I think I'm in the clear.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.734
Pterodactyls
also are not dinosaurs.

00:00:48.734 --> 00:00:50.968
They're pterosaurs.

00:00:50.968 --> 00:00:52.334
I feel like in 2015,

00:00:52.334 --> 00:00:53.934
when they were doing
all this reclassification,

00:00:53.934 --> 00:00:55.667
though, they might have said
about pterodactyls --

00:00:55.667 --> 00:00:57.133
The scientists could have
gotten together and said,

00:00:57.133 --> 00:00:58.634
"Do we need the P?"

00:00:58.634 --> 00:01:01.567
[ Laughter ]

00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:04.000
What are -- Who are we
trying to impress?

00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:09.667
We had a sketch with a crossbow
this week,

00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:12.200
and we mentioned...

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.334
that it shot an arrow.

00:01:14.334 --> 00:01:16.300
Crossbows do not shoot arrows.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:20.267
They shoot bolts or quarrels.

00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:23.467
And it was very surprising
that we had an error

00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:27.267
because that was a sketch that
our writer Ben Warheit wrote.

00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:32.634
It's very uncommon for there
to be an error...

00:01:32.634 --> 00:01:34.801
in his sketches.

00:01:34.801 --> 00:01:38.067
He has a nickname in the
writers' room -- "Kid Details."

00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:39.901
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:47.000
Well...

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.801
I mean, the worst
of the corrections

00:01:48.801 --> 00:01:50.701
are the ones that,
you know, recur.

00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:55.467
And once again, I said "less"
when I should have said "fewer."

00:01:55.467 --> 00:01:57.501
And I got to say,
this is the heartbreaking thing.

00:01:57.501 --> 00:01:59.300
It was in "Corrections"
last week,

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:00.701
and I'm gonna play it for you.

00:02:00.701 --> 00:02:03.334
Watch in real time
as I catch myself,

00:02:03.334 --> 00:02:04.968
and I'm so worried
I'm doing it wrong,

00:02:04.968 --> 00:02:07.934
I correct myself incorrectly.

00:02:07.934 --> 00:02:09.934
I get it wrong.
Watch.

00:02:09.934 --> 00:02:12.868
Here it is last week,
me saying it wrong.

00:02:12.868 --> 00:02:15.968
I also was told I did...

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:20.934
two less "Guess who's backs."

00:02:20.934 --> 00:02:23.501
I mean, it just -- the fact that
you've told me so many times

00:02:23.501 --> 00:02:26.000
and in the moment...

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.501
I get it wrong again...

00:02:28.501 --> 00:02:31.968
I think really proves that
this whole endeavor is folly.

00:02:34.234 --> 00:02:37.601
I was talking about
"Without Me,"

00:02:37.601 --> 00:02:39.067
the classic Eminem track.

00:02:39.067 --> 00:02:42.167
And I fell into the jackal trap.

00:02:42.167 --> 00:02:44.567
I jackal'd the lyrics
to that song a little ba--

00:02:44.567 --> 00:02:46.000
a little bit, excuse me,

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.267
about the fact that
I've always found it confusing

00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:53.634
that the song starts,
"Guess who's back.

00:02:53.634 --> 00:02:56.868
Back again. Shady's back.
Tell a friend.

00:02:56.868 --> 00:02:58.434
Guess who's back, guess who's
back, guess who's back."

00:02:58.434 --> 00:03:01.133
And I'm saying, "Why do you
keep asking us who's back,

00:03:01.133 --> 00:03:04.334
to guess who's back, no less,
when you told us?"

00:03:04.334 --> 00:03:05.734
And then people said that,

00:03:05.734 --> 00:03:08.100
"You're not listening
to 'tell a friend.'"

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:11.901
Those are then the friends
passing on "Guess who's back."

00:03:11.901 --> 00:03:15.400
And...I'm embarrassed
and I'm ashamed.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.601
I'm embarrassed I got it wrong,
and I'm ashamed because

00:03:18.601 --> 00:03:20.968
I think the reason I did that,
the reason I was nitpicky

00:03:20.968 --> 00:03:22.934
is I...

00:03:22.934 --> 00:03:26.634
hold a grudge, a small grudge,
with Eminem.

00:03:26.634 --> 00:03:28.033
[ Light laughter ]

00:03:28.033 --> 00:03:31.200
I am a huge fan of Eminem.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.934
To the point that I wrote him
a couple of letters.

00:03:33.934 --> 00:03:35.968
[ Laughter ]

00:03:38.634 --> 00:03:41.801
Never heard back.
Six months I waited.

00:03:41.801 --> 00:03:44.601
He got them.

00:03:44.601 --> 00:03:46.567
I wrote the address perfect.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.100
I don't want to defend Eminem's
titular character, Stan,

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.067
but I will say,
at least he took the time

00:03:57.067 --> 00:03:58.934
to air his grievances
via letter,

00:03:58.934 --> 00:04:03.801
unlike you, who just type it
into a comments section.

00:04:03.801 --> 00:04:08.634
Be so ni--
One of you take the time...

00:04:08.634 --> 00:04:11.367
to write a nice letter.

00:04:11.367 --> 00:04:12.801
You're just firing it off,

00:04:12.801 --> 00:04:16.968
often with worse grammar than
the grammar you claim I'm using.

00:04:20.834 --> 00:04:24.300
Unpopular opinion,
"Stan" is the best

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.133
of the epistolary raps.

00:04:29.701 --> 00:04:31.467
I will say, I can't believe I'm
going to do this right around,

00:04:31.467 --> 00:04:35.934
turn around and criticize
another Eminem lyric choice.

00:04:35.934 --> 00:04:38.968
But in the end --
the last verse of "Stan,"

00:04:38.968 --> 00:04:40.534
so are we to expect --

00:04:40.534 --> 00:04:43.400
So Eminem's writing
the letter to Stan.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.467
And then we hear
a pencil scratching.

00:04:46.467 --> 00:04:48.200
In real time, he figures out

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:52.467
this is the Stan
he just saw on the news.

00:04:52.467 --> 00:04:58.133
And he writes, "Oh, wait.
That was you. Damn."

00:04:58.133 --> 00:05:01.033
Are we -- So in the Eminem
rap-o-matic universe,

00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:04.200
is the idea that he --
at Stan's house,

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:10.534
there's an unopened letter
that Eminem finished, "Damn,"

00:05:10.534 --> 00:05:13.033
and then signed?

00:05:13.033 --> 00:05:14.901
These are the answers
I'm looking for.

00:05:17.067 --> 00:05:20.467
We said only a lunatic
would drink Diet Dr Pepper,

00:05:20.467 --> 00:05:22.767
and...

00:05:22.767 --> 00:05:24.834
two things we got in
the comments section this week

00:05:24.834 --> 00:05:29.000
are dinosaur experts
and Diet Dr Pepper drinkers.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.801
'Cause a lot of you said
it's a wonderful drink.

00:05:30.801 --> 00:05:32.234
And no hard feelings.

00:05:32.234 --> 00:05:35.434
I do want to prove
that it was just a joke

00:05:35.434 --> 00:05:37.868
and we did not mean any --

00:05:42.934 --> 00:05:45.133
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.934
We were --

00:05:53.934 --> 00:05:55.501
It was in reference...

00:05:59.901 --> 00:06:02.734
...to...

00:06:02.734 --> 00:06:04.234
to Sarah Palin.

00:06:04.234 --> 00:06:06.534
And...

00:06:06.534 --> 00:06:07.901
we...

00:06:07.901 --> 00:06:10.567
[ Laughter ]

00:06:10.567 --> 00:06:13.667
Every time Sarah Palin comes up
in the news, I --

00:06:13.667 --> 00:06:17.734
it makes me reflect on...

00:06:17.734 --> 00:06:20.734
a very special time
for me at "SNL."

00:06:20.734 --> 00:06:22.601
Shoemaker and I were there,
2008.

00:06:22.601 --> 00:06:24.367
It was a really cool time
to be there,

00:06:24.367 --> 00:06:26.501
really cool group
of cast and writers.

00:06:26.501 --> 00:06:30.634
And I wrote a sketch
for Amy and Tina

00:06:30.634 --> 00:06:33.601
where they played Sarah Palin
and Hillary Clinton.

00:06:33.601 --> 00:06:37.567
But, as with all political
sketches at the time,

00:06:37.567 --> 00:06:40.033
we were always looking for
other people to pitch jokes.

00:06:40.033 --> 00:06:44.701
And if there is a most famous
line in that sketch,

00:06:44.701 --> 00:06:47.567
it is, "I can see Russia
from my house."

00:06:47.567 --> 00:06:49.567
And I did not --

00:06:49.567 --> 00:06:51.534
Shoemaker was the one
who pitched it.

00:06:51.534 --> 00:06:52.667
I remember
like it was yesterday.

00:06:52.667 --> 00:06:53.868
We were sitting
with the "Update" group,

00:06:53.868 --> 00:06:57.067
and Shoemaker said --
he pitched that line.

00:06:57.067 --> 00:06:59.434
And then I wrote it.
I wrote it down right away.

00:06:59.434 --> 00:07:02.534
So I wrote -- I wrote it.

00:07:02.534 --> 00:07:05.634
He said it, I wrote it.

00:07:05.634 --> 00:07:09.701
But that is --
It does speak to, for as...

00:07:09.701 --> 00:07:14.501
irascible and unpleasant
he is to be around,

00:07:14.501 --> 00:07:16.334
Shoemaker is a great producer

00:07:16.334 --> 00:07:18.033
because he makes everything
a little bit better.

00:07:18.033 --> 00:07:19.400
And that's a perfect example,

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:20.901
because that was
the best line in the sketch

00:07:20.901 --> 00:07:22.167
and it came from Shoemaker.

00:07:22.167 --> 00:07:24.701
He's been dining out
on it, though.

00:07:24.701 --> 00:07:26.234
[ Laughter ]

00:07:26.234 --> 00:07:29.767
Not a day goes by.

00:07:29.767 --> 00:07:33.400
And the most embarrassing
thing is he still...

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:35.267
[ Sighs ]

00:07:35.267 --> 00:07:38.834
He still pitches versions of it
all the time.

00:07:38.834 --> 00:07:41.334
Like, it was perfect for that.

00:07:41.334 --> 00:07:42.534
But, like, yesterday,

00:07:42.534 --> 00:07:44.467
we're writing "A Closer Look"
about Matt Gaetz,

00:07:44.467 --> 00:07:46.167
and Shoemaker's just lying
on a couch still.

00:07:46.167 --> 00:07:49.100
I think he's fine,
but he's like, "Oh, my hip.

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:50.400
I need the whole couch."

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.601
So, like, everybody --
other writers are standing,

00:07:52.601 --> 00:07:58.200
and he goes,
"What if Matt Gaetz says,

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:02.400
'You know what I can see
from my house? Russia.'"

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:03.667
What?

00:08:03.667 --> 00:08:06.167
[ Light laughter ]

00:08:06.167 --> 00:08:07.868
Just the worst joke.

00:08:11.334 --> 00:08:15.067
Someone told me a martini
with vodka is called a Gibson.

00:08:15.067 --> 00:08:17.033
That's incorrect.
Not to correct a jackal.

00:08:17.033 --> 00:08:22.334
It's a -- A Gibson is a martini
with a pickled onion.

00:08:22.334 --> 00:08:26.834
It's the nighttime drink
of Diet Dr Pepper drinkers.

00:08:26.834 --> 00:08:28.901
[ Laughter ]

00:08:34.367 --> 00:08:36.701
I mentioned the film
"The Ice Storm,"

00:08:36.701 --> 00:08:38.400
I think this week.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.067
And a wonderful film,

00:08:40.067 --> 00:08:42.000
directed by Ang Lee,
an incredible cast.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:44.434
And one member of the cast
I left out was Elijah Wood,

00:08:44.434 --> 00:08:47.601
who, as always in everything,
was so great in that.

00:08:47.601 --> 00:08:48.934
And a lot of people said --

00:08:48.934 --> 00:08:50.667
accused me, even,
of leaving him out

00:08:50.667 --> 00:08:53.501
because he's also in
"The Lord of the Rings" films.

00:08:53.501 --> 00:08:56.200
And obviously, I have
a complicated relationship

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:57.334
with those films.

00:08:57.334 --> 00:08:59.000
The complication is,
I have stuff to do.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:02.701
So when I watch a movie,
it's like...

00:09:02.701 --> 00:09:06.434
"Pick up the pace.
There's a life outside this."

00:09:09.901 --> 00:09:12.267
But that's not why
I left him out.

00:09:12.267 --> 00:09:16.400
Alright, so I'm gonna give
a spoiler to "The Ice Storm."

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:18.567
So if you haven't seen it,
pause it, watch it now,

00:09:18.567 --> 00:09:22.200
or maybe just skip ahead,
let's say, two minutes.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:24.267
Elijah Wood's character
dies in "The Ice Storm."

00:09:24.267 --> 00:09:28.133
And that's fascinating
because he plays Frodo, right?

00:09:28.133 --> 00:09:30.534
Makes it through a trilogy

00:09:30.534 --> 00:09:33.767
and then one more movie
as Frodo, lives.

00:09:33.767 --> 00:09:35.000
And that's the problem.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.434
There's just no stakes
in "Lord of the Rings."

00:09:38.434 --> 00:09:42.334
Like, to make it through
Middle Earth but not...

00:09:42.334 --> 00:09:44.100
New Canaan, Connecticut.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:46.467
[ Laughter ]

00:09:46.467 --> 00:09:48.767
Like, nobody dies in
"Lord of the Rings," right?

00:09:48.767 --> 00:09:50.200
I know I'm going to hear it.

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:52.968
I mean, Gandalf does, but then
he, like, comes right back.

00:09:52.968 --> 00:09:55.067
As, like, Gandalf the White.
Am I supposed to be sad?

00:09:55.067 --> 00:09:57.200
He just came back
with a different shirt.

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:00.934
It's like coming home.
"Dad's dead."

00:10:00.934 --> 00:10:03.300
"What?"
"Oh, he got a new sweater."

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:05.367
[ Laughter ]

00:10:09.334 --> 00:10:11.934
I mean, I guess I was sad
when Sméagol died.

00:10:11.934 --> 00:10:13.501
[ Deep voice ]
No, you weren't.

00:10:13.501 --> 00:10:15.667
[ Laughter ]

00:10:20.133 --> 00:10:22.734
[ Normal voice ]
Oh, the letters don't turn
on "Wheel of Fortune" anymore.

00:10:22.734 --> 00:10:25.767
They're screens.

00:10:25.767 --> 00:10:27.567
I should have known that,
but I haven't been home sick

00:10:27.567 --> 00:10:30.133
for a while.

00:10:30.133 --> 00:10:31.901
I think we have our winning mug.

00:10:31.901 --> 00:10:36.133
We got to our final two,
and I think they match up well.

00:10:36.133 --> 00:10:38.367
We could have gone
with these two,

00:10:38.367 --> 00:10:39.767
but I think these are
our two mugs, right?

00:10:39.767 --> 00:10:41.467
We got this on one side --

00:10:41.467 --> 00:10:42.834
"Late Night
with Correction Jackals,"

00:10:42.834 --> 00:10:46.801
and then we have that jackal
at a computer.

00:10:46.801 --> 00:10:48.667
So I think this is --
we're going to try to get this

00:10:48.667 --> 00:10:50.767
for sale at the store,
hopefully you all --

00:10:50.767 --> 00:10:54.167
And also, Tara told me
that when we first showed this,

00:10:54.167 --> 00:10:57.634
a lot of you thought it looked
more like a hyena than a jackal.

00:10:57.634 --> 00:11:00.434
And I like that it
makes you unhappy.

00:11:00.434 --> 00:11:02.634
[ Laughter ]

00:11:05.467 --> 00:11:07.501
Asterisk.

00:11:07.501 --> 00:11:09.667
I said it wrong last week,

00:11:09.667 --> 00:11:13.267
and the way I pronounced it
definitely was incorrect.

00:11:13.267 --> 00:11:15.701
But a lot -- There's this thing
where you accuse me

00:11:15.701 --> 00:11:16.968
of doing something on purpose.

00:11:16.968 --> 00:11:18.634
It was not on purpose.

00:11:18.634 --> 00:11:22.434
It wasn't to set up...some bit.

00:11:22.434 --> 00:11:25.968
I just said "asterisk" wrong.
I just flubbed it.

00:11:25.968 --> 00:11:27.501
I pronounced it asta--

00:11:27.501 --> 00:11:29.801
[ "Never Gonna Give You Up"
plays ]

00:11:29.801 --> 00:11:36.100
♪♪

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:37.434
Astley-rick.

00:11:37.434 --> 00:11:39.501
[ Laughter ]

00:11:46.267 --> 00:11:49.601
I mentioned my book, which I...

00:11:49.601 --> 00:11:51.167
I guess at this point
I'm bragging about a little bit.

00:11:51.167 --> 00:11:56.267
First book -- First children's
book ever to have...

00:11:56.267 --> 00:11:58.601
a bear and a rabbit.

00:11:58.601 --> 00:12:01.067
A lot of people
keep saying that's wrong.

00:12:01.067 --> 00:12:04.434
And somebody sent me this
and said, "What about this?"

00:12:04.434 --> 00:12:06.367
This is from a movie, bro.

00:12:08.567 --> 00:12:11.400
It's not from a book.

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:16.334
Then I do weekend "Today" show
with Willie Geist,

00:12:16.334 --> 00:12:17.434
our friend Willie Geist.

00:12:17.434 --> 00:12:20.667
And they have great researchers.

00:12:20.667 --> 00:12:22.367
And I'm watching.

00:12:22.367 --> 00:12:25.634
And look at this.
Can you guys read that?

00:12:25.634 --> 00:12:29.734
"'I'm Not Scared, You're Scared'
is first children's book."

00:12:29.734 --> 00:12:32.701
[ Laughter ]

00:12:35.968 --> 00:12:39.501
I mean, I just assumed.

00:12:39.501 --> 00:12:42.100
Because it seems like
such an obvious idea.

00:12:45.234 --> 00:12:47.000
But, I mean,
I guess all the best --

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:49.167
That's what the best ideas are,
right?

00:12:49.167 --> 00:12:51.767
You hear them
and you're like, "Ohh."

00:12:51.767 --> 00:12:53.400
That's quite an honor.

00:12:56.467 --> 00:13:02.734
I have been reading reviews
of the book.

00:13:02.734 --> 00:13:04.634
And mostly positive,
but you go on Goodreads,

00:13:04.634 --> 00:13:09.968
and someone gave it
three stars out of five

00:13:09.968 --> 00:13:12.267
and said it was cute but wordy,
and I get it.

00:13:12.267 --> 00:13:15.968
Not all parents want their kids
to learn all the words.

00:13:18.734 --> 00:13:20.567
There's twists, though.

00:13:20.567 --> 00:13:23.200
Like, this book --
There's a story.

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:27.267
Like, it's wordy because there
are things that happen.

00:13:27.267 --> 00:13:28.601
There's more plot, I think,

00:13:28.601 --> 00:13:31.100
than you would find
in a normal kids' book,

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:32.834
and I'm happy about it.

00:13:32.834 --> 00:13:35.234
The bear learns
to confront fear,

00:13:35.234 --> 00:13:38.300
and you find out at the end
that the whole time the rabbit

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:42.200
has been Russian-born
German heiress Anna Delvey.

00:13:46.501 --> 00:13:47.968
And I think the best moment
in the book

00:13:47.968 --> 00:13:52.234
is when she says to Bear [As
Delvey] "Do you know who I am?"

00:13:52.234 --> 00:13:55.467
I've been trying to get my Anna
Delvey impression into the show,

00:13:55.467 --> 00:13:56.767
and I feel like
the window's closing,

00:13:56.767 --> 00:13:58.234
and I didn't ever want it
to seem forced.

00:13:58.234 --> 00:14:00.200
And I thought
that was pretty organic.

00:14:02.167 --> 00:14:04.100
[ As Delvey ]
I'll get your money, Bear.

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:07.167
[ Laughter ]

00:14:07.167 --> 00:14:10.067
The European banks are closed.

00:14:13.667 --> 00:14:16.400
The -- Oh, we have another...

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:19.701
[ Laughter ]

00:14:23.067 --> 00:14:28.033
We have another E.B. White
letter to share.

00:14:30.634 --> 00:14:33.033
[ Light laughter ]

00:14:33.033 --> 00:14:34.601
Ah.

00:14:37.868 --> 00:14:39.501
Another E.B. White letter.
This is really exciting.

00:14:39.501 --> 00:14:41.367
Also, I heard from
a lot of archivists --

00:14:41.367 --> 00:14:43.367
"archive-ists?" --

00:14:43.367 --> 00:14:46.133
that I did a lot wrong last week
when I took out the letter.

00:14:46.133 --> 00:14:48.767
You're supposed to use cotton
gloves, if any gloves at all.

00:14:48.767 --> 00:14:50.434
They say it's maybe better
not to use gloves.

00:14:50.434 --> 00:14:53.200
You just have to have clean,
sterilized hands

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:55.567
because you actually want
the tactile feeling

00:14:55.567 --> 00:14:56.968
with an old letter
to make sure --

00:14:56.968 --> 00:14:58.501
sometimes when you get gloves,
you'll maybe be

00:14:58.501 --> 00:15:00.100
a little rough with it.

00:15:00.100 --> 00:15:05.167
So clean, sterilized hands
and then don't use tweezers

00:15:05.167 --> 00:15:06.501
because that could
damage the paper.

00:15:06.501 --> 00:15:08.000
Certainly don't use tweezers
on the gloves

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:09.234
because that could put
a hole in them.

00:15:09.234 --> 00:15:11.067
Don't blow in the gloves
because that gets, like --

00:15:11.067 --> 00:15:15.434
That puts the germs inside.
I did a lot wrong.

00:15:15.434 --> 00:15:19.567
But we keep hearing from this,
you know, the little girl, Kay,

00:15:19.567 --> 00:15:22.167
who wrote the original letter to
E.B. White

00:15:22.167 --> 00:15:24.000
saying she didn't like
the ending,

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:25.534
and then he wrote back to her
and said

00:15:25.534 --> 00:15:27.000
that he did like the ending.

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:29.000
And her son has reached
out very kindly

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:31.601
and shared these letters
with us.

00:15:31.601 --> 00:15:33.868
And so this is a trip.

00:15:33.868 --> 00:15:36.167
I believe the original letters
were in 1953.

00:15:36.167 --> 00:15:40.501
So now it's 13 years later.
It's 1966.

00:15:40.501 --> 00:15:46.501
And Kay Berthold, who is...

00:15:46.501 --> 00:15:47.767
now 13 years older --

00:15:47.767 --> 00:15:49.567
She reads an article
in "The New York Times" --

00:15:49.567 --> 00:15:51.767
this is real -- about --
and by the way,

00:15:51.767 --> 00:15:55.701
when I say "this is real,"
everything's real.

00:15:55.701 --> 00:15:58.000
It's a tic.

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:00.400
So there was an article
in "The New York Times"

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:02.934
about how librarians had said

00:16:02.934 --> 00:16:04.300
"Stuart Little"
was bad for children.

00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:05.968
And so he sent us, again,

00:16:05.968 --> 00:16:08.601
this is a copy of the letter
that his mom,

00:16:08.601 --> 00:16:12.334
Kay Berthold, wrote in 1966
and basically,

00:16:12.334 --> 00:16:14.267
"I was very interested to read
your article

00:16:14.267 --> 00:16:16.000
about 'Stuart Little'
in the Sunday "New York Times"

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:17.701
on March 6th, 1966, entitled

00:16:17.701 --> 00:16:19.133
'Librarians Said
It Was Bad for Children.'

00:16:19.133 --> 00:16:20.334
I was one of those children

00:16:20.334 --> 00:16:21.968
who expressed dislike
for the book's ending.

00:16:21.968 --> 00:16:23.701
When I read 'Stuart Little'
about age 10,

00:16:23.701 --> 00:16:26.000
I loved it and yet
was troubled by the ending.

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:27.534
You answered my letter

00:16:27.534 --> 00:16:29.968
saying that it was important
not that Stuart found Margalo,

00:16:29.968 --> 00:16:31.434
but that he was headed
in the right direction.

00:16:31.434 --> 00:16:33.834
Now, 13 years later,

00:16:33.834 --> 00:16:37.234
I'm a Peace Corps volunteer
in Uruguay.

00:16:37.234 --> 00:16:38.767
'Stuart Little' is still
one of my favorite books,

00:16:38.767 --> 00:16:41.234
but more important is the letter
you sent me as a child.

00:16:41.234 --> 00:16:43.200
I was thrilled.
Now I say thank you.

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:47.868
So that is the letter that
his mom sent to Stuart Little,

00:16:47.868 --> 00:16:49.501
and once again --
not to Stuart Little,

00:16:49.501 --> 00:16:52.567
to E.B. White.
Excuse me.

00:16:52.567 --> 00:16:55.701
Once again, E.B. White --
what a lovely man

00:16:55.701 --> 00:16:57.367
who writes back.

00:16:57.367 --> 00:16:59.534
So here is the letter --

00:16:59.534 --> 00:17:02.067
you see, on "New Yorker"
letterhead --

00:17:02.067 --> 00:17:04.601
and it says,
"Dear Miss Berthold,

00:17:04.601 --> 00:17:05.834
it was very good of you
to write,

00:17:05.834 --> 00:17:07.234
and I'm glad the 'Times' piece
interested you.

00:17:07.234 --> 00:17:09.334
I still receive letters
of complaint from children

00:17:09.334 --> 00:17:11.534
about the book's ending,
and I'm sure I always will.

00:17:11.534 --> 00:17:12.901
So it was an extra pleasure

00:17:12.901 --> 00:17:15.267
to have your retrospective
report.

00:17:15.267 --> 00:17:16.400
From your address, I should say

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:17.901
you were headed
in the right direction.

00:17:17.901 --> 00:17:22.567
I hope the going isn't too rough
and that the rewards are great.

00:17:22.567 --> 00:17:25.100
Along with about
180 million others,

00:17:25.100 --> 00:17:26.934
I send thanks and good wishes.

00:17:26.934 --> 00:17:28.934
Sincerely, E.B. White."

00:17:28.934 --> 00:17:31.667
And it made me feel bad
because obviously, you know,

00:17:31.667 --> 00:17:36.133
we've had some laughs
at the expense of Mr. White,

00:17:36.133 --> 00:17:38.701
who wrote these beautiful
children's books,

00:17:38.701 --> 00:17:41.367
and the fact
that he would write back

00:17:41.367 --> 00:17:44.501
to her twice is really special,

00:17:44.501 --> 00:17:46.234
13 years later.

00:17:46.234 --> 00:17:48.968
But then I wanted to get
the original, right?

00:17:48.968 --> 00:17:52.868
So I call my friends at
the Cornell University Library,

00:17:52.868 --> 00:17:54.934
and they're like,
"That's not a real letter."

00:17:54.934 --> 00:17:57.334
They said this is --
this isn't real.

00:17:57.334 --> 00:17:58.801
And they go,
"But we have a real --

00:17:58.801 --> 00:17:59.901
we have the real response."

00:17:59.901 --> 00:18:02.567
So...

00:18:02.567 --> 00:18:04.934
we've got it right here.

00:18:04.934 --> 00:18:07.267
And...

00:18:07.267 --> 00:18:09.501
Cotton -- cotton gloves.

00:18:11.601 --> 00:18:17.300
Get it right this time
because I heard from my friends,

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:19.767
but then do you remember
what I said?

00:18:19.767 --> 00:18:21.667
We don't wear gloves

00:18:21.667 --> 00:18:27.000
because we want the tactile
feeling of no gloves.

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:28.601
So we take them off.

00:18:28.601 --> 00:18:31.601
[ Laughter ]

00:18:31.601 --> 00:18:37.567
And then we -- we sterilize
our hands with,

00:18:37.567 --> 00:18:39.701
I guess, some sort of alcohol.
This will work.

00:18:39.701 --> 00:18:43.367
[ Laughter ]

00:18:43.367 --> 00:18:45.434
There we go. That should do it.

00:18:49.133 --> 00:18:54.067
And now this is
the actual letter

00:18:54.067 --> 00:18:57.834
from E.B. White.

00:18:57.834 --> 00:19:00.734
Okay, see,
this is very clearly --

00:19:00.734 --> 00:19:02.667
because it has the letterhead,

00:19:02.667 --> 00:19:05.734
and there's some fire damage,
which means it's old.

00:19:05.734 --> 00:19:07.901
[ Laughter ]

00:19:07.901 --> 00:19:12.601
And it's -- you can also tell
it's been in some tea.

00:19:12.601 --> 00:19:14.334
So it says, "Dear Miss Berthold,

00:19:14.334 --> 00:19:15.601
it was very good of you
to write,

00:19:15.601 --> 00:19:17.100
and I'm glad the 'Times' piece
interested you.

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:18.601
I still receive letters
of complaint from children

00:19:18.601 --> 00:19:21.234
about the book's ending" --
so it starts the same.

00:19:21.234 --> 00:19:25.167
"One particularly bothersome
girl wrote me in 1953 to say,

00:19:25.167 --> 00:19:26.567
"I didn't like the ending
because

00:19:26.567 --> 00:19:28.200
I don't know if he's going to
get there or not."

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:30.601
Come to think of it,
her name was --

00:19:30.601 --> 00:19:33.000
It was you. Damn."

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:35.868
[ Laughter ]

00:19:49.734 --> 00:19:51.167
Um...

00:19:55.133 --> 00:19:56.367
I want to clear
one last thing up.

00:19:56.367 --> 00:20:00.667
I talked about Jimmy Reilly...

00:20:00.667 --> 00:20:05.200
last week,
who I asked Shoemaker --

00:20:05.200 --> 00:20:09.100
I said, "What is his title?"
And I was told head carpenter,

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:11.400
which is not what Jimmy
has ever told me.

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:14.634
[ Laughter ]

00:20:14.634 --> 00:20:17.167
But I guess the show wouldn't
put

00:20:17.167 --> 00:20:19.334
"Head mother [bleep] in charge"
in the credits.

00:20:19.334 --> 00:20:21.634
[ Laughter ]

00:20:23.601 --> 00:20:27.767
And I joked about how you can't
run afoul of Jimmy

00:20:27.767 --> 00:20:29.067
or the Reilly family

00:20:29.067 --> 00:20:31.100
because they're this
secret cabal that, you know,

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:34.000
run 30 Rock
and pull the levers of power.

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:38.667
Look, I was joking, okay?
I'm --

00:20:38.667 --> 00:20:41.934
This is my show, okay?

00:20:41.934 --> 00:20:44.868
For all the jokes about
Shoemaker is in charge

00:20:44.868 --> 00:20:47.000
or, you know
this person is in charge,

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:48.734
I'm in charge, okay?

00:20:48.734 --> 00:20:52.000
I run the ship here.

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:54.701
I'm not afraid
of the Reilly family.

00:20:54.701 --> 00:20:57.467
[ Laughter ]

00:21:01.701 --> 00:21:03.367
Well, that...

00:21:03.367 --> 00:21:06.834
Can they do that, though?

00:21:06.834 --> 00:21:08.467
Is it...

00:21:08.467 --> 00:21:11.133
Well, isn't...

00:21:11.133 --> 00:21:14.400
Well, can anybody else do it?

00:21:14.400 --> 00:21:15.501
But isn't that Kathleen?

00:21:15.501 --> 00:21:18.167
[ Cellphone ringing ]

00:21:20.367 --> 00:21:22.567
Hello?

00:21:22.567 --> 00:21:24.767
Well, I was -- I was kidding.

00:21:24.767 --> 00:21:27.167
[ Laughter ]

00:21:27.167 --> 00:21:30.534
Yeah, well, we're just in
the middle of it, and it's...

00:21:30.534 --> 00:21:31.701
No, I-I -- no.

00:21:31.701 --> 00:21:33.400
I didn't even think
you were here today.

00:21:35.634 --> 00:21:38.400
No, I -- It was just a joke.
I don't...

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:40.667
Okay.

00:21:40.667 --> 00:21:44.067
Well, can you turn them back on?

00:21:44.067 --> 00:21:47.267
Well, I am -- I am going to
think about what I did.

00:21:47.267 --> 00:21:49.434
[ Laughter ]

00:21:51.033 --> 00:21:53.267
Do you want me to take
the whole hiatus?

00:21:55.033 --> 00:21:57.133
No.

00:21:57.133 --> 00:22:00.200
The whole thing?

00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:01.868
All right.

00:22:03.801 --> 00:22:07.000
[ Laughter ]

00:22:15.334 --> 00:22:17.267
[ Exhales shakily ]

00:22:19.234 --> 00:22:20.133
[ Belches loudly ]

